'''Belton House''' is a [[Grade I]] listed [[English country house|country house]] in the parish of [[Belton, South Kesteven|Belton]] near [[Grantham]] in [[Lincolnshire]], England, built between 1685 and 1687 by [[Sir John Brownlow, 3rd Baronet]]. It is surrounded by formal gardens and a series of avenues leading to [[folly|follies]] within a larger wooded [[Deer park (England)|park]]. Belton has been described as a compilation of all that is finest of [[Restoration style|Carolean]] architecture, said to be the only truly [[Vernacular architecture|vernacular]] style of architecture that England had produced since the [[Tudor period]]. It is considered to be a complete example of a typical English country house; the claim has even been made that Belton's principal façade was the inspiration for the modern British motorway signs which give directions to [[stately home]]s.

For about three centuries until 1984, Belton House was the [[Family seat|seat]] of the [[Brownlow baronets|Brownlow family]], which had first acquired land in the area in the late 16th century. Their heirs, the Cust family, were created [[Baron Brownlow]] in 1776. Despite his great wealth Sir John Brownlow, 3rd Baronet, chose to build a comparatively modest house rather than one of the grand [[Baroque architecture|Baroque]] palaces being built by others at the time. The contemporary, if provincial, Carolean style was the selected choice of design. Nevertheless, the new house was fitted with the latest [[innovation]]s, such as [[sash window]]s for the principal rooms, and followed the latest thinking on house-planning, in seeking to separate those parts of the building that were for the use of the family from the areas where servants carried out their domestic duties. Successive generations made changes to the interior of the house which reflected their changing social position and tastes, yet the fabric and design of the house changed little.

In August 1914, Belton House and its park were used as the assembly point for the newly-formed [[11th (Northern) Division]] before its deployment in [[World War I]], and in October 1915 it was used as the home depot and training ground of the [[Machine Gun Corps]]. During [[World War II]], [[RAF Belton Park]] was established in the grounds of the house, as were two RAF Regiment squadrons, by November 1944 1,850 personnel were based at Belton.

The Custs, like many previously wealthy English families, were faced with mounting financial problems. Edward Cust, 7th Baron Brownlow opened the estate to the public. An adventure playground was built in the nearby woods to attract families to the house as a tourist attraction. However, the financial difficulties were too great and in 1984 they donated the house, with most of its contents, to the [[National Trust]]. The trust introduced new features and attractions to fund repairs and conservation. Further revenue is raised from the use of the property as a filming location, and from licensing the Marble Hall for civil weddings. It was visited by 419,982 people during 2024.

==History==
[[File:Alice Brownlow, née Sherard (1659-1721), by John Riley.jpg|thumb|Portrait of Alice Brownlow, née Sherard, by [[John Riley (painter, born 1646)|John Riley]]|alt=see caption]]

The [[Brownlow baronets|Brownlow family]], a dynasty of [[lawyers]], began buying property in [[Lincolnshire]] to augment their income in 1598. Among these properties was the [[Estate (land)|manor]] of [[Belton, South Kesteven|Belton]], {{cvt|2|mi}} distant from [[Grantham]]. [[Richard Brownlow]], who established the family's wealth, began negotiations to acquire the manor from Sir Henry Pakenham in 1603 and secured its [[Reversion (law)|reversion]] to him six years later. The Pakenhams hosted King [[James VI and I|James I]] in the manor for a night in 1617. This occasion financially ruined the Pakenhams and led to the resignation of their interest in the property to Brownlow in exchange for a lifelong [[annuity]].{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|pp=5–6}}{{efn|This annuity was paid until 1641, when Sir Henry Pakenham's wife, who survived him by 20 years, died.{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|p=6}}}} Brownlow neither much stayed at nor made much change to the Belton manor, preferring to reside at other properties. When he died, Brownlow was succeeded by his son [[Sir John Brownlow, 1st Baronet]], who himself died childless in 1679. John Brownlow had, however, become attached to two of his more distant blood relations: a great-nephew, also called [[Sir John Brownlow, 3rd Baronet|John Brownlow]], and a great-niece, [[Alice Sherard, Lady Brownlow|Alice Sherard]], who married in 1676 when both were aged 16.{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|p=7}}

After inheriting the estates of their great-uncle, Alice and John Brownlow sought to enter [[London]] high society. To that end, they bought a [[town house]] in the newly fashionable [[Bloomsbury Square|Southampton Square]] in [[Bloomsbury]] and decided to build a new country house at Belton to flaunt their wealth. In 1684 they acquired labourers and materials for the house, hiring brickmakers, bricklayers, and stonemasons, and carefully demolished the old manor, which stood near the present [[orangery]], to recycle its components. The [[cornerstone]] of what became Belton House was laid on 23 March 1685 and its exterior was largely completed by the latter half of the following year. The interiors of the house were completed in 1687.{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|pp=6–8, 11–12}}

===Private residence===
The Brownlows moved into Belton House in November&nbsp;1688.{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|p=12}} On 29&nbsp;October&nbsp;1695, they hosted [[William III of England|William III]], who was reported to have enjoyed his stay so much that he was too [[hung over]] to eat any of the food provided on his visit to [[Lincoln, Lincolnshire|Lincoln]] the following day. Two years later, in July&nbsp;1697, Sir John Brownlow II committed suicide at a relative's residence in [[Dorset]]. Ownership of Belton House passed to his brother, William, who was content to permit Alice to remain in occupation until her death in 1721, and she spent that time arranging advantageous marriages for her five daughters. One of those marriages was between her youngest daughter, Eleanor, and [[John Brownlow, 1st Viscount Tyrconnel|Sir John Brownlow III]], William's son. John III, made [[Viscount Tyrconnel]] in 1718, inherited Belton House on Alice's death and refurnished the house.{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|pp=15–18}}

[[File:Leighton Brownlow.jpg|left|thumb|Adelaide, wife of the 3rd Earl Brownlow, in a portrait by [[Frederick Leighton]]|alt=see caption]]

Sir John Brownlow III also died childless in 1754, and like Sir John Brownlow I focused his energies on a nephew, also named [[Sir John Cust, 3rd Baronet|John]], son of his sister Anne and Sir Richard Cust.{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|p=20}} The [[Cust baronets|Cust family]] had family ties to the slave trade.{{sfn|Huxtable|2020|pp=33, 80}}{{efn|The Custs' links to the slave trade were principally through John Cust's younger brother, [[Edward Cust|Edward]], whose wife, [[Mary Anne Cust|Mary Boode]], came from a Dutch family with extensive [[slave plantation]]s in the [[West Indies]].{{sfn|Huxtable|2020|p=80}} On the passing of the [[Slavery Abolition Act 1833]], the Cust brothers both received compensation for the loss of their slaves; John received some £5,000 as a trustee.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.ucl.ac.uk/lbs/claim/view/8908|title=British Guiana 716A & B (Greenwich Park) – Claim details|publisher=UCL Centre for the Study of the Legacies of British Slavery|access-date=25 January 2023}}</ref>}} They moved into a house in Grantham and received the Viscount Tyrconnel's patronage. Upon John III's death, Anne inherited Belton House and lived in it until 1766, when she gave it to John Cust—elected [[Member of parliament|MP]] for Grantham in 1743 and then [[Speaker of the House of Commons (United Kingdom)|Speaker of the House of Commons]] from 1761 to 1770—to provide him a residence befitting his political station.{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|p=20}} Cust died in 1770, because of the "unusual fatigues" of his office, according to his monument at Belton, and Belton passed to his son, [[Brownlow Cust, 1st Baron Brownlow|Brownlow Cust]], who was created [[Baron Brownlow]] in 1776.{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|pp=20–21}}

Brownlow Cust made sweeping changes to Belton House. First, from 1770 to 1771, he arranged for repairs to its interiors and then hired architects [[James Wyatt]] and John Langworth to modernise the house over the rest of the century. In 1807, Brownlow Cust died and was succeeded by his son, [[John Cust, 1st Earl Brownlow|John]], created Earl Brownlow in 1815.{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|pp=21–24}} The Earl also made changes to Belton House and its grounds, employing [[Jeffry Wyatville]], James Wyatt's nephew, from 1809 to 1820 and [[Anthony Salvin]] in the 1830s.{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|pp=24–27}}{{sfn|Pevsner|Harris|Antram|1989|pp=135–36}} On his death in 1853 he was succeeded by a grandson, who himself died in 1867 leaving Belton House, and the manor and estate of [[Ashridge]] in [[Hertfordshire]], inherited in 1849 by the 1st Earl's oldest son, [[John Egerton, Viscount Alford]], to another grandson, [[Adelbert Brownlow-Cust, 3rd Earl Brownlow]].{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|pp=27–29}}<ref>{{NHLE|num=1348442|desc=Ashridge House|grade=I|access-date=27 January 2023}}</ref>

Although the 3rd Earl preferred to live at Ashridge or at [[Carlton House Terrace]] in London, he spent the rest of the 19th century reverting Belton House to its 17th-century appearance.<!--{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|p=30}}--> The house entered the 20th century in a good state of repair and preservation, but that century was to present Belton and its estate with serious problems. In January 1921, the 3rd Earl died childless. As a consequence, his title became extinct and his estates were inherited by Adelbert Salusbury Cockayne-Cust, 5th Baron Brownlow. The diminishing value of the family's land and [[death duties]] for the 3rd Earl obliged the 5th Baron to sell Ashridge and its art collections.{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|pp=30, 33–34}} Further death duties were incurred on Adelbert's death in 1927,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nationaltrustcollections.org.uk/object/436159.1|title=Adelbert Salusbury Cockayne Cust, 5th Baron Brownlow (1867–1927)|publisher=National Trust|access-date=27 January 2023}}</ref> who passed the house to his son, [[Peregrine Cust, 6th Baron Brownlow|Peregrine]].{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|p=34}} The 6th Lord Brownlow was involved in the 1936 [[abdication of Edward VIII]] as his [[Lord-in-waiting]].{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|p=34}}{{sfn|Thornton|1986|p=137}} Edward VIII, who became the [[Duke of Windsor]] after the abdication, visited Belton in the 1930s with his mistress [[Wallis Simpson]], whom Brownlow tried in vain to persuade to stay at Belton House in the course of the crisis.{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|p=34}}

===Belton House and the World Wars===
Belton Park held temporary encampments for the training of [[British Army]] units for many years before the start of [[World War I]], but had never held any permanent facilities.{{sfn|Greaves|2014|p=32}} At the beginning of the war, like many other British landowners, the 3rd Earl Brownlow offered the house and grounds of Belton and Ashridge to the Government for war service. The offer was accepted and in August&nbsp;1914, Belton House and its park were used as the assembly point for the newly-formed 11th (Northern) Division before its deployment. A small town was erected on the Belton estate for the soldiers by April&nbsp;1915 and included such amenities as a [[YMCA]], a cinema and its own railway line.<ref name="NT-BEL-WW1">{{Cite web|title=Belton House and the First World War|url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/visit/nottinghamshire-lincolnshire/belton-house/belton-house-and-the-first-world-war|publisher=[[National Trust]]|access-date=30 January 2023}}</ref> In October&nbsp;1915, the home depot and training ground of the [[Machine Gun Corps]] were established in the southern part of Belton park.<ref name="NT-BEL-WW1"/>{{sfn|Greaves|2014|pp=32–33}} By 1922, the camp had closed and all the military buildings had been demolished.{{sfn|Greaves|2014|pp=32–33}} An archaeological dig at the site was undertaken by the ''[[Time Team]]'' in 2012.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Belton House|url=https://www.wessexarch.co.uk/our-work/belton-house|website=[[Wessex Archaeology]]|date=November 2012|access-date=24 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220128170120/https://www.wessexarch.co.uk/our-work/belton-house|archive-date=28 January 2022}}</ref>

The years following World War I were severely testing for the owners of many great estates. Both indoor and outdoor staff had previously been plentiful, essential, and cheap, but were now in short supply. Millions of men had left private service to join the army, and very few returned.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/magazine-19544309|first=Lucy|last=Wallis|title=Servants: a life below stairs|publisher=BBC Magazine|date=22 September 2012|access-date=25 January 2023}}</ref> Female domestic staff had been called up for war service in factories, and now realised there was an easier and better paid existence outside the great country houses.<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.cam.ac.uk/research/news/who-mops-the-floor-now-how-domestic-service-shaped-20th-century-britain|first=Lucy|last=Durlap|title=Who mops the floor: home domestic service shaped 20th-century Britain|publisher=University of Cambridge|date=28 July 2011|access-date=25 January 2023}}</ref>

Belton again saw war service during World War II. In March 1942 the [[regimental depot|depot]] for the [[RAF Regiment]] was established at the park as [[RAF Belton Park]]. It was housed in [[Nissen hut]]s.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.newarkairmuseum.org/RAF_Regiment|title=Royal Air Force Regiment Display|publisher=Newark Air Museum|access-date=26 January 2023}}</ref>{{sfn|Oliver|2002|p=8}} Alongside the depot, Belton Park also held the [[RAF Regiment]] Officer Cadet Training Unit (OCTU) which provided officers with the professional skills necessary for service in the regiment.{{sfn|Oliver|2002|p=8}} Two RAF Regiment squadrons were formed at Belton Park in 1942; 2788 Field Squadron in March and 2774 Field Squadron in May.{{sfn|Oliver|2002|p=49}} By November 1944 1,850 personnel were based at Belton.{{sfn|Hancock|1985|p=57}} When the war ended in the following year the RAF Regiment was reorganised and Belton Park became No. 1 Depot, continuing on as the OCTU. The RAF Regiment left Belton in August 1946, closing its depots and sub-depots in Lincolnshire.{{sfn|Hancock|1985|p=59}}

===National Trust===
Following the wars, many thousands of country houses of great architectural value were demolished, or had whole wings razed to the ground.{{sfn|Robinson|2011|p=11}} In 1955 alone one house was demolished every five days.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.architecture.com/WhatsOn/Exhibitions/AtTheVictoriaAndAlbertMuseum/Room128a/2006/SAVE/SAVEBritainsHeritage.aspx|title=SAVE Britain's Heritage 1975–2005: 30 Years of Campaigning|website=architecture.com|publisher=Royal Institute of British Architects|access-date=5 May 2009|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080724095841/http://www.architecture.com/WhatsOn/Exhibitions/AtTheVictoriaAndAlbertMuseum/Room128a/2006/SAVE/SAVEBritainsHeritage.aspx|archive-date=24 July 2008}}</ref> In this respect Belton was fortunate to survive at all, as in addition to the family's problems, the house deteriorated to such an extent that in 1961 the 6th Baron employed the architect [[Francis Johnson (architect)|Francis Johnson]] to oversee a restoration program lasting three years.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://archiveshub.jisc.ac.uk/search/archives/e19cc90a-9be9-3536-8c3f-2ddd041b1d97?terms=Belton%20House&component=347b3845-9e87-3024-9086-683a2b253929|title=Francis Johnson Archive – Belton House|publisher=University of Hull|access-date=26 January 2023}}</ref> The roof was replaced, much of the panelling taken down and repaired, and new cornices installed. Attempts were also made to curtail serious infestations of [[dry rot]].{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|pp=34–35}} In the previous decade, Belton was designated a [[Listed building|Grade I listed building]], the highest possible grade reserved for structures of exceptional interest.<ref name="auto">{{NHLE|num=1235523|desc=Belton House|grade=I|access-date=25 January 2023}}</ref> [[Gervase Jackson-Stops]], the architectural historian suggests, in his 1990 study ''The Country House in Perspective'', that the main façade of the house was the inspiration for the [[Road signs in the United Kingdom|British motorway signs]] ([[Image:HH icon.png]]) which indicate the proximity of [[stately home]]s.{{sfn|Jackson-Stops|1990|p=56}}
[[File:Belton Stables.jpg|thumb|upright=1.5|The stables, restored by the Trust in 2016 as the restaurant|alt=see caption]]
The seventh Baron attempted to retain the house and estate by opening to the public. An adventure playground was built in the nearby woods to attract families to the house as a tourist attraction. However, the financial difficulties were too great and in January 1984 he transferred ownership of the house, garden, and some of the contents to the [[National Trust]], a charitable body experienced in the management of historic properties. An auction of the contents was held at Belton House by [[Christie's]] over three days 30 April – 2 May 1984, comprising 1,022 lots.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://thecarycollection.com/products/belton-house-lincolnshire|title=Christie's 1984 Sale Catalogue|publisher=Cary Collection|access-date=26 January 2023}}</ref> The National Trust then purchased {{cvt|1,317|acres|km2 ha}} of parkland and much of the remaining contents at a cost of £8 million (worth about £{{formatprice|{{inflation|UK|8000000|1984|r=-6}}}} today) with a grant from the [[National Heritage Memorial Fund]].{{sfn|Marsden|1985|pp=6, 57}}{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|p=35}}{{efn|The contents, comprising just under 20,000 items of art, antiques, silver and other artefacts, represented some four hundred years of collecting by the Brownlow family.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nationaltrustcollections.org.uk/place/belton-house|title=Belton House Collection|publisher=National Trust|access-date=29 January 2023}}</ref> A modern example is a portrait by the African-American artist [[Richmond Barthé]], bought by the 6th Earl, who had a holiday home in Jamaica when Barthé was resident on the island.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nationaltrustcollections.org.uk/object/436186|title=Seated Man in a Landscape (Lucian Levers) – Item NT436186|publisher=National Trust|access-date=29 January 2023}}</ref> The sitter has recently been identified as Lucian Levers.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://news.artnet.com/art-world/a-painting-hidden-away-in-a-u-k-country-manor-turns-out-to-be-a-rare-canvas-by-the-harlem-renaissance-artist-richmond-barthe-2187758|first=Vittoria|last=Benzine|title=A painting hidden away in a U.K. country manor turns out to be a rare canvas by the Harlem Renaissance artist Richmond Barthé|publisher=Artnet|date=6 October 2022|access-date=29 January 2023}}</ref>}}

A priority for the Trust on their taking over Belton was the establishment of a restaurant, to augment the estate's income, and encourage people to spend more time at Belton and travel greater distances to visit.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/visit/nottinghamshire-lincolnshire/belton-house/eating-and-shopping-at-belton|title=Eating and shopping at Belton|publisher=National Trust|access-date=26 January 2023}}</ref>{{efn|Following a major restoration begun in 2016, the restaurant is now housed in William Stanton's stable block, one of only twenty-one Grade I listed stables in England.<ref name="auto1">{{NHLE|num=1235620|desc=Stables to Belton Park|grade=I|access-date=27 January 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/visit/nottinghamshire-lincolnshire/belton-house/belton-house-stables-project|title=Belton's Stables project|publisher=National Trust|access-date=27 January 2023}}</ref>}} Though the house, its contents, and outbuildings were in an adequate state of repair at the time of the gift, they have since become part of an ongoing programme of conservation and restoration.<ref>{{cite web|url= https://rodneymelvilleandpartners.com/projects/country-houses/belton-house/|title=Projects – Belton House|publisher=Rodney Melville & Partners|access-date=26 January 2023}}</ref> At the same time, the National Trust has introduced new features and attractions such as a silver exhibition that displays a collection of silver amassed by the Brownlow family, dating from 1698.{{sfn|Tinniswood|1992|p=41}} Further revenue is raised from the use of the property as a filming location, and from licensing the Marble Hall for civil weddings.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/belton-house/hire-this-venue/ |title=Weddings at Belton House |publisher=The National Trust |access-date=1 October 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121028222225/http://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/belton-house/hire-this-venue/ |archive-date=28 October 2012 }}</ref> The house has featured in the [[Moondial (TV serial)|1988 TV adaptation]] of the 1987 children's novel ''Moondial''; as "Rosings Park" in the [[BBC]]'s [[Pride and Prejudice (1995 TV serial)|1995 television version]] of ''[[Pride and Prejudice]]''.<ref>{{Cite magazine|last=Wainwright|first=Penny|title=Belton House and 'Moondial'|url=https://www.lincolnshirelife.co.uk/culture/belton-house-and-moondial/|magazine=Lincolnshire Life|date=November 2017|access-date=24 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220627141922/https://www.lincolnshirelife.co.uk/culture/belton-house-and-moondial/|archive-date=27 June 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Behind the Scenes: Choosing the Locations|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/drama/prideandprejudice/behindthescenes/pp_locations_feature.shtml|work=Drama: Pride and Prejudice|publisher=BBC|date=24 September 2014|access-date=18 August 2018}}</ref> and in the Netflix series ''[[Bridgerton]]''.<ref>{{cite web |title=Bridgerton's backdrop 'comes alive' after refurb |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c3rz3v1ng3lo |website=BBC News |access-date=7 March 2026}}</ref> It was visited by 419,982 people during 2024.<ref name="ALVA 2024 visitor numbers">{{cite web |title=ALVA – Association of Leading Visitor Attractions |url=https://www.alva.org.uk/details.cfm?p=423|website=www.alva.org.uk |access-date=2 December 2025}}</ref>

==Architecture==
